Toll_Description:
- Toll on the river Ems, first mentioned in 1224. Toll reductions were introduced in 1457 for citizens of Groningen, who were freed from staple and toll obligations in Emden; in 1493 for Hamburg beer; and in 1497, reduced toll rates are mentioned for merchants from M&uuml;nster. East Frisians were exempted from the Ems toll as well. The toll rates were doubled in 1541.&lt;br/&gt;
- The 1494 staple privileges contained a so-called &#039;ruderzoll&#039; for large see-going vessels that entered the harbour of Emden in cases of emergency, but were not obliged to stay in the town for a certain time.&lt;br/&gt;
- New tolls were introduced in 1595 for imported commodities belonging to East Frisians, or being transported further to East Frisia. They were cancelled between 1603 and 1605. Citizens from Emden were exempted from these tolls in 1610.

