1) Already in 877, an Imperial toll for ships hauled upstream on the Rhine existed in Neuss. Pfeiffer claims that this toll was market-related. A Rhine toll of the bishop of Cologne is mentioned in 1138. The towns of Cologne and temporarily Rees were exempted from the toll. The Rhine toll was moved to Zons because of changes in the course of the river in 1372. In 1243, at least 6 denarii per ship had to be paid. In 1236 about 1200 Cologne mark were collected, in 1256 about 800 Cologne mark. For further tariffs and revenues, see Pfeiffer. &lt;br/&gt;
2) A market toll is mentioned in 1138 and 1169, and in 1203 a transit toll for land traffic of the bishop of Cologne. At least between 1389 and 1393, a horse toll was levied by the bishop, and a toll for safe conduct was granted by king Wenceslaus in 1396. In 1396, 2 to 3 alb. per horse had to be paid, depending on the load. In the years 1389/93, an average of about 2152 mark per year could be collected. Half of the land toll was acquired by the town of Neuss in 1455; in 1463 completely. In 1455, about 700 Rhenish guilders were collected. Elaborate details about the practice of levying the toll are known from testimonies written during a 1478-1479 conflict about the toll between D&uuml;sseldorf and Neuss. To prevent circumventing of the toll station, the toll was levied at many secondary toll stations around town as well. Detailed list of toll tariffs are known from 1601 and 1618.&lt;br/&gt;
3) The town of Neuss also levied a road toll in 1373. However, the Archbishop of Cologne declared this to be illegitimate, which is why its existence after 1373 is not known.

